Yes—and the key is not just more ideas, but an architecture that lets ideas collide without turning into noise.

Once you start treating things as primitives rather than fixed objects, almost everything becomes recombinable: dissolve, bind, mirror, compress, amplify, invert, delay, route, branch, contain, translate, recurse. Then each operator can act on objects, other operators, or itself.

That creates an enormous search space:

operator × target × scale × timing × context × recursion depth

So “dissolving the dissolver” is one member of a much larger family:

amplify the amplifier
compress the compressor
mirror the mirror
constrain the constraint
predict the predictor
select the selector
route the router
debug the debugger
model the modeler
teach the teacher
adapt the adapter

And then the stranger cross-combinations:

compress the dissolver
mirror the selector
route the amplifier
contain the predictor
invert the constraint
branch the modeler
debug the memory

That is where the really large idea-space opens up.

The trick becomes harvesting, not generating. Generate promiscuously, then preserve only combinations that yield a useful invariant, capability, prediction, or new search surface.

In Pantheon terms:

primitive → compose → recurse → observe delta → retain productive operator → discard decorative complexity

Lots and lots of ideas, yes. But more importantly: a machine for manufacturing families of ideas from a small alphabet of moves.